  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Your phone does not need to remember a perfect photograph of your finger. It learns a pattern of distinguishing details. Many phones use a capacitive sensor to read ridges and valleys. Tiny electrical differences help map where each ridge sits. The system finds features such as ridge endings and splits. It stores a mathematical template, not an ordinary image you can open. When you try again, the new scan becomes another template. The phone compares features and their positions allowing for small shifts. A close match unlocks, a poor match fails. So your fingerprint becomes a pattern the phone can compare without expecting two touches to be identical.",
